Subject: Internet Billing Servers; Digital Signature Standard

I've heard several times of people setting up an Internet Billing
Server (IBS) that would make it easy to pay for information services
provided over the net, but I don't know of any implementations.  Are
there any implementations?  Are there any serious barriers that
prevent implementations?

An obvious obstacle is secure interactions with the IBS.  Given the
present environment, I don't expect a high-profile IBS operating in
the US using public key cryptography to provide secret transactions to
work.  However, for all I know, digital signatures might be legal.
Are there implementations of the Digital Signature Standard (DSS)
available?  Would such an implementation be usable to provide
non-private but non-forgeable interaction with the IBS?  Are there
obstacles to exporting a DSS implementation out of the US, or sending
digitally signed documents into or out of any country?

(I'm assuming we use paper mail for key distribution, since I'm
assuming that cryptographic privacy is unavailable.)

Is this the proper forum for these questions?

