Date: Mon, 3 Jan 1994 13:22:10 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Internet Billing Servers; Digital Signature Standard

Tim Freeman:
> Are there any implementations [of IBS]?

No. There are a wide variety of proposals floating around: Phil 
Trubey's IBS, digital checks, digital postage, NetCash, and various 
flavors of offline and online digital cash.  The closest to
implementation may be the heavyweight EDI (Electronic Document
Interchange), which is being ported to the Internet from X.* by
LLNL.  EDI handles contracts billing, among other things, between major 
corporations and their suppliers, between the U.S. government and its 
contractors, etc.  EDI is probably not suitable for small business 
in its native form, and it's not clear whether one could write software 
to make it sufficiently freindly.

Doug Barnes and Jim McCoy are considering implemenenting digital
cash as "MOO-money" for metaverse.io.com as a testbed to get
out the most obvious bugs in a harmless enviroment.  Plans are also 
afoot for serious Internet-based banking.

> Are there any serious barriers that
> prevent implementations?

A major sales job is needed to bring various BBS's on to the Internet,
and to get small Internet services to farm out their billing operations.
An anonymous billing service using digital cash requires a fairly complex, 
difficult to test protocol.  Net reliability is a major
barrier for online billing.  None of these are insurmountable.

> Given the
> present environment, I don't expect a high-profile IBS operating in
> the US using public key cryptography to provide secret transactions to
> work.  

RSAREF is perfectly legal, as is ViaCrypt PGP; PGP itself
may run afoul of patent laws.  There would be problems distributing 
crypto code outside the U.S & Canada, though.  Query talk.politics.crypto
or the cypherpunks list for details and the status of DSS.

> Is this the proper forum for these questions?

imp-interest-request@thumper.bellcore.com is dedicated to discussing the
business aspects of IBS and digital cash; cypherpunks-request@toad.com 
discusses cryptographic issues including the technical details and
legal status of public key crypto, digital cash, and the like.
See sci.crypt for general crypto technical issues.

