as a virtual seminar. (Many other extropians will have no interest at all
in this topic and will undoubtedly accuse me of being a wimp-simp and
kvetch about the increase in the list-noise, but that's life.)

I'll collect the replies and write the essay later.

I will only quote anyone if they have specifically allowed for me to do so.
Everyone else will remain anonymous for the purposes of the essay. If you
really want anonymity try sending me private e-mail using a cypherpunk
remailer or some other anonymity service. Does anyone know if there is a
cypherpunk remailer faq around somewhere?

The essay will be copylefted.

__________________________________________
A few thoughts on extropian relationships:

To me, any relationship is an extropian relationship if it is based on
rational self-determination. As individualists, it is neccesary to reject
the limitations and expectations of the usual cultural definition. In my
opinion, this redefinition towards self-oriented values is path to
Transhumanism, and what extropianism is all about. A relationship, by
definition, is defined by the people involved. To attempt to twist it to
conform to some preconceived notion is often disastrous.

When I use the term "reject the limitations and expectations of the usual
cultural definition" here, I mean simply a rejection of the restriction
that the relationship MUST follow the traditional pattern. For some
relationships the traditional happens to be the best.

As a note, even tho' my conversion to extropianism has been fairly recent,
my handling of love relationships has followed this pattern for more than a
decade. Go figure.

It is still a constant battle to defy the cultural condition that we have
received over our lives. I do not doubt that I, and other extropians,
occasionally succumb to these unconscious desires to conform. This is one
of the reasons I feel it is important to discuss these matters.

Rejecting predefined conceptions becomes a particular problem when in a
relationship with a non-extropian, who expects a relationship to follow a
certain pattern. My own attempts at this have not been particularily
successful (at least not in the long run). Usually, I succeed in
intellectually convincing them that the relationship is defined by the
people involved, but later, during the definition phase, they are shocked
to hear that I would even consider a non-monagamous situation. Many never
get past this revulsion of "difference" and refuse to believe I could ever
be happy in a so-called "normal" relationship. There are times when candor
is not a comfortable thing.

There are others on this list who are having more success at this
(relationships with non-extropians) and I invite them to share their
thoughts.

Since the two-person monogomous relationships are the cultural norm, I'm
mostly interested in talking about larger, less monogomous relationships.
This is what I some times refer to as the "Free Market of Love".
Competetive pressures certainly keep you from taking the relationship for
granted.

Some of the non-traditional options and topics to consider:

1> Free "Love" - Love usually meaning sex in this context.

2> Discriminating free love - Promiscuity is definite health risk these days.

3> Open relationships - Primary Relationships with no restrictions on
creating secondary relationships. 

4> Polyfidelity & Group Marriages - If you can manage to find this many
mutually compatable people, more power to ya. I'm happy to just find one,
but she thinks a 4 person marriage is optimal. Having experience in a
couple of triads, I've found this type of relationship to be fairly
unstable (but then again, so were the people involved). Recent discussions
have pointed out the advantages of this arrangement for child rearing.
Unfortunately many countries outlaw this practice.

5> Marriages & Other Social contracts (Extropians-in-law): Should the state
be involved in marriage contracts? (All together now: NO!) How do others
avoid this? Also, other types of social contracts in love relationships,
like cohabitation contracts.

6> Dealing with emotional scars (both yours and those of your mate(s)) from
previous relationships (and from cultural damage).

7> Conflict resolution - I'm personally really big on communication (mostly
preventative) and "working it out".

8> Gender (and other sexual) preferences and how they affect the above. As
Le Marquis Plaz d'Ominibus (my nym d'amour & guerre) would paraphrase
Fermat, "I have a really good sexual preference, but it's description is
too large to fit in this space."

More later...

