Date: Mon, 3 Jan 1994 19:17:53 CST  
From: "Kathleen Cadmus" <kcadmus@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: No More Prisons

Several recent posters about imprisonment have strayed far from what I
conclude to be the only view consistent with Extropian principles.
Imprisonment should be totally abolished!

There seem to be four justifications for imprisonment:

    1) Isolation
    2) Rehabilitation
    3) Restitution
    4) Retribution

I now proceed (IMHO :-)) to shoot them all down.

1) Isolation -

I will not address the gross impropriety of our existing legal codes. Let's
keep this discussion theoretically sound by assuming a minimal set of legal
proscriptions against the usual crimes against self and property including
breach of contract. From my own perspective, everyone has some non-zero
probability of future crimes against me. Should I imprison you all to keep
myself safe from you? Maybe only those with such a probability beyond a
given threshold level? (Is this the justification for locking up a drunk
driver despite the lack of demonstrable harm done?) We are on the
slipperiest of slopes here. Is the crime rate higher than average in the
Black community? Lock them all up?

Sorry but we all need to accept the responsibility of protecting our selves
and our possessions *without* resorting to the coercive acts which we claim
to deplore. What is more anti-extropian than placing another human being in
a cage? Protection from possible future criminal acts is a feeble excuse for
this scaredy-cat statist behavior.

2) Rehabilitation -

Rehab services are available in the marketplace. I have heard no reasonable
arguments for forcing anyone into unwanted rehabilitation. Why not just go
all the way and lobotomize the sons-of-bitches? Sounds like rehab to me. To
entertain such concepts is, again, to flirt with becoming a statist goon.

Note that I have no argument with a market for rehab centers. The enrollment
would be voluntary and paid for by the enrollee or sponsor. I would hope
that they would be One-Step centers rather then Twelve-Step centers. The one
step? --> "Cut it the fuck out or prepare to die." Simple enough, huh?

3) Restitution -

Great concept, of course, but what does it have to do with imprisonment?? If
some dude steals my chicken or kills my child, he owes me restitution. How's
he gonna do that in prison? But I hear you objecting that how else do we
ensure that the restitution is paid? Weel, why not the same way we ensure
payment of any other debts. Extropians are well aware of many
anarcho-capitalist schemes for solving this problem so I won't expand on it
here. Protection against failed extraction of restitution is simply a
variation on justification number 1 which was demolished above.

4) Retribution -

Aha! This is what you all really want! You're pissed off and want sanction
to commit your own criminal acts. And I say -- "grow up and learn to control
your emotions!" The concept of retributive justice is trashed elegantly in
the book "Without Guilt and Justice" by Walter Kaufmann. This highly
recommended book includes also includes a sparkling explication of morality
which I find to be very attuned to extropianism. The highest morality
consists of autonomy and the escape from Decidophobia. I can summarize on
the list if there is any interest. (Other fascinating books by Kaufmann
include "Critique of Religion and Philosophy" and "Nietzsche: Philosopher,
Psychologist, Antichrist".)

Asides aside, I believe imprisonment is a barbaric institution which has no
place in an extropian future.

Fire (flame) away!
